---
// The plain-language opening: what the move actually is, and the one rule.
//
// Layer 1 of the two the page carries. It exists so the team can understand
// what they are approving without reading an operator's checklist, and it says
// the reassuring thing first — the site is built; this is only about the
// address — because the fear this page has to answer is that cutover means
// rebuilding something.
import EmailRule from './EmailRule.astro';
---
<section id="what">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What this actually is</h2>
    <div class="lede">The site is already built. This is only about pointing the address at it.</div>
    <p class="sub">
      <code>harvardintech.com</code> is served today by Strikingly, the no-code tool
      the current site was built in. The replacement site is finished and running —
      it's the one you've been reviewing. Nothing about it changes at cutover. What
      changes is a handful of settings at the domain registrar, so that typing
      <code>harvardintech.com</code> lands on the new site instead of the old one.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      The technical work is genuinely small. The care is all in sequencing: doing
      the reversible parts first, proving each one before the next, and keeping the
      old site alive underneath the whole time so there is always a way back.
    </p>

    {/*
      The "nothing has happened yet" banner used to be hardcoded here. It is
      now <RunbookSummary /> at the top of the page, because the state it
      asserts is real: with ticks committed, a fixed line claiming nothing
      has happened sits directly under a count saying four steps are done.
      One statement of where we are, derived from the file — not two, one of
      which is a guess that goes stale the first time anyone ticks anything.
    */}

    <EmailRule />
  </div>
</section>
